{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was a member of the House of Toulouse and the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III was born around 1140 and succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\nRaymond III was a vassal of the King of Jerusalem and played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. In 1153 he was involved in the siege of Ascalon and in 1157 he led a military campaign against the Fatimid Caliphate. Raymond III also participated in the Battle of Inab in 1149 where he was captured by the Zengid forces.\n\n*Conflict with the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\nRaymond III had a complicated relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. In 1164 he was captured by the Zengid forces during the Battle of Harim and was imprisoned for a year. After his release Raymond III began to consolidate his power in Tripoli and established good relations with the Byzantine Empire. He also supported the claims of the Ibelin family to the throne of Jerusalem.\n\n*Later Life and Death*\nRaymond III died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin where he fought alongside King Guy of Lusignan against the forces of Saladin. He was succeeded as Count of Tripoli by his nephew Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. Raymond III's death marked the end of the Toulouse dynasty in Tripoli and the beginning of a new era in the county's history.\n\n*Succession and Legacy*\nRaymond III's legacy is complex and multifaceted. He played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Crusader states. His relationships with the Byzantine Empire and the Ibelin family had significant consequences for the region. Despite his conflicts with the Kingdom of Jerusalem Raymond III remained a loyal vassal and fought alongside the King during the Battle of Hattin.\